questions.corrige_markdown:
**(i) Hazard and evidence (2 marks).** The coastline is vulnerable to **coastal flooding (from high tides, storm surges and waves, made worse by sea-level rise) and coastal erosion**. Evidence from the map (any two, 1 mark for the hazard and 1 for the evidence):
- The land along the shore is low and gently sloping: very few contours are drawn near the coast and the first contour shown is the 50 m line, so a wide strip of land lies only a few metres above sea level.
- Settlements, the main coast road, the Oil Refinery with its jetty, Bota Land, Wovia, Mokundange, Ngeme Village and the built-up edge of Limbe Town (Botanical Garden, Ambas bay) are right on the water's edge, so people and property are exposed.
- Beaches and a much indented coast with small bays (Mortan bay, Ambas bay) show active wave action, which erodes and floods the shore; swamp and mangrove symbols in the east show that the ground is regularly wet.

**(ii) Two measures (2 marks, 1 each, stated and explained).** Any two:
- Build sea walls, embankments or groynes (and rock revetments) to stop waves and high tides reaching the settlements and the refinery;
- Plant and protect mangroves and other coastal vegetation, which absorb wave energy and bind the soil;
- Forbid building within a set-back distance from the shore (zoning) and move the most exposed houses to higher ground;
- Improve drainage and raise the level of roads and key installations; set up a flood early-warning and evacuation plan; nourish the beaches with sand.
